8.44.030 – General regulations.

A. Littering and Improper Disposal.

1. No person may shall throw, drop, or deposit refuse upon any street, alley, sidewalk, or
any yard, or otherwise upon any premises, public or private;.

2. No person may shall place refuse upon another person's private property;.

3. No person shall place accumulations of household, business, or construction waste in
containers or dumpsters owned or rented by other individuals, businesses, or
government entities;.

3. 4. No person may shall place refuse in any street, alley, or other public place except
in proper containers for collection or under upon the express written approval granted
by the director of the public works director. department; and

5. No person shall throw or deposit any solid waste in any stream or other body of water
within the city.

B. The owner of the any property where waste is generated is shall be responsible for the
disposal of the waste regardless of whether the owner resides on or otherwise occupies the
property.

C. The owners owner or occupants occupant of any store or other place of business situated
within the city will shall exercise reasonable diligence at all times to keep their business
premises clean of refuse, such as wastepaper, wrapping paper, paper napkins, cartons,
package containers, glass bottles, broken glass fragments, cups, cans, tobacco product
waste, and other use or other waste materials thrown or left on the premises by its business
customers or any other person, and shall take reasonable measures to prevent the same
from blowing to adjoining premises.

D. If owners or occupants fail a business owner or occupant fails to keep their premises or an
adjoining premises clean, as required under subsection (C) of this section, the city may is
authorized to arrange for cleanup of the premises and may bill the owner for the cost of the
service. Receptacles of sufficient size and number must shall be placed on the premises,
and be accessible to customers of the business or any other person on the premises, for the
disposal of waste.
